## Iteration 4 — 2026-10-04 17:08:35
Item **03 Fonts and colours**: SOLVED.

### Completed
- **Tests first, and they failed fir

Item 03 (fonts and colours) is done and marked `[x]`. The gate passed: 1518 tests in 7:37, and `racket/` is unchanged. Eight items are still open, so there's no `LOOP_COMPLETE` line. I haven't committed anything.

**The Coderack question:** in Qt, the `i`s and `l`s render. The cause on the Tk side is that the Tk tkinter loads here (Anaconda's) is built without Xft (Linux's antialiased-font library). It draws with old X core fonts, which the X server turns into black-and-white bitmaps from Type 1 font files. At 8, 9 and 10 pixels those bitmaps give the thin strokes of `i` and `l` no pixels; at 7 and 11 pixels they survive. The default Coderack is 598 pixels high, so its labels are exactly 8 pixels. A real screen with this same Tk would lose them too. I put a side-by-side comparison in `docs/screenshots/panels/small-text-tk.png` and `small-text-qt.png` (I looked at both), and updated the anomalies entry to "explained". The tkinter GUI is left as it is.

**What changed:**
- **Font mapping:** a new file, `qt/fontspec.py`, reads Tk font descriptions the way Tk 8.6 does, in all their forms, and gives Tk's own error messages. The canvas now rejects a bad font as Tk does.
- **Text measurement:** `qt/fonts.py` measures with the same Qt font it draws with.
  - Widths: 97% of the sample widths are exactly Tk's. The worst case is 22-pixel bold Helvetica, up to 6 pixels apart, because the two programs round the letters differently.
  - Heights: Qt's own font heights were up to 7 pixels taller than Tk's. I now take them from the letters' actual extents, the way X core fonts work. All heights are now within 3 pixels of Tk's, and the baseline is within 1.
- **Panels use the Qt fonts:** a new `install()` makes the panels' font code choose its fonts from what Qt has and measure text on a Qt canvas. Item 04 needs to call it at startup.
- **Text tolerance:** the allowed difference between Qt's and Tk's text boxes in `test_qt_canvas.py` went from 6 pixels to 2.
- **Colours:** all 752 colour names in `colors.py` are read as Tk reads them, and Qt paints exactly those colours. Five standard names (gray, grey, green, maroon, purple) come out with Tk's values rather than `colors.py`'s; that was already logged in item 02.
- **Tests:** written first in `python/tests/test_qt_fonts.py`; the first run gave 40 failed, 24 passed. The Tk reference data was recorded under Xvfb into `python/tests/data/tk-fonts-colors.json`.

**One visible difference you might not expect:** the Qt GUI picks Liberation Serif for the "serif" font where the tkinter GUI uses Nimbus Roman. That's because fontconfig maps the original's first choice, "times new roman", to Liberation Serif. The two fonts have about the same letter widths, but serif text sits a little differently from the tkinter screenshots. It's recorded in `docs/divergences.md`, along with the antialiased text.

All of this is written up in `PROGRESS.md` (iteration 4), `docs/anomalies_and_quirks.md`, `docs/qt-gui-plan.md` and the two test/screenshot READMEs.

